1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Stretching over hundreds of square miles along the Gulf of Mexico is the Louisiana Delta.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
As day breaks, the stillness is shattered by the sound of approaching combat.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
This great body of water is a bass angler's field of dreams

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:25,000
and a fitting arena for one of the biggest tournaments in fishing, the Sitco Bassmasters Classic.

5
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:32,800
Over the next three days, a field of 45 anglers will compete for a first prize of $100,000.

6
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:40,720
With attendant endorsements, winning the Classic can net the champion the equivalent of a million dollars.

7
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,900
Alright guys, everybody, Juan.

8
00:00:50,901 --> 00:00:56,060
The road to the Classic begins at hundreds of regional tournaments across the country,

9
00:00:56,061 --> 00:01:02,160
where the prizes may be much smaller, but the rules of engagement are the same.

10
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:07,540
A flotilla of specially designed boats takes to the water.

11
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:14,400
The signal is given, and the battle is joined.

12
00:01:14,401 --> 00:01:16,400
We're clear.

13
00:01:16,401 --> 00:01:18,400
We're clear.

14
00:01:18,401 --> 00:01:20,400
We're clear.

15
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:29,400
Using an arsenal of technology, the players must figure out where the fish are,

16
00:01:29,401 --> 00:01:34,400
then get as many pounds of bass as possible into the boat before the closing horn.

17
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,400
I came out here on Wednesday to pre-fish,

18
00:01:40,401 --> 00:01:45,080
pre-fish and I was checking another fellow out there that was fishing with

19
00:01:45,081 --> 00:01:50,180
live bait and I watched him catch three really nice fish and I told my partner

20
00:01:50,181 --> 00:01:54,260
this morning you know I'd like to go there let's see what happens we started

21
00:01:54,261 --> 00:01:58,280
catching these four and five pound fish and it turned out it was a great day

22
00:01:58,281 --> 00:02:03,740
each angler has his own game plan or strategy the fishing is pretty slow today

23
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:09,460
so we've sized down we're using light line small worms and just dragging them

24
00:02:09,461 --> 00:02:10,393
very slowly.

25
00:02:10,394 --> 00:02:16,460
Well, today we're Carolina rigging, which is the weight on top with a bead and

26
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:21,722
a long leader and the worm down below it.

27
00:02:21,723 --> 00:02:24,820
And this will kick up some mud and then that'll

28
00:02:24,821 --> 00:02:31,900
get the fish's attention and then the worm will come back behind it.

29
00:02:31,901 --> 00:02:35,633
The contestants will cast with rods made of high strength graphite.

30
00:02:35,634 --> 00:02:36,580
Their reels, made

31
00:02:36,581 --> 00:02:42,540
from space age materials are filled with monofilament line that is virtually invisible underwater.

32
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,080
But ultimate success today will depend on what is attached to the end of the line.

33
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:56,440
Victory will belong to the man or woman who can accurately match the fishing conditions

34
00:02:56,441 --> 00:03:01,180
to the proper offering from his or her treasure chest of amulets and charms.

35
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:05,100
The time-honored and much revered Tacklebox.

36
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,780
For example, this box here contains crankbaits.

37
00:03:09,781 --> 00:03:14,640
Another one you might see in a tackle box is a spinnerbait.

38
00:03:14,641 --> 00:03:17,080
All bass fishermen throw spinnerbaits.

39
00:03:17,081 --> 00:03:24,380
You'll have spinners, surface, crank, and most importantly, there's no tackle like the

40
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:29,540
good old-fashioned worm box.

41
00:03:29,541 --> 00:03:33,200
And the worm box are going to have their own special worms that they always find to catch

42
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:36,200
fish.

43
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:40,200
Clearly the thrill of competition isn't the only reason people love to fish.

44
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:45,380
Today there are over 30 million licensed anglers in the U.S. alone.

45
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:48,660
So where does this fishing fever come from?

46
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,200
Is it simply a pleasant escape from the pace and pressures of our modern lives?

47
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,740
Or is there some deeper, more primordial connection?

48
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:03,200
Back to a time when one's fishing skill was often the difference between survival and starvation.

49
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:11,800
Most historians agree that fishing appeared somewhat later than its older cousin, hunting.

50
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:17,760
The spear and the woven trap were probably the first tackle used.

51
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:25,520
The woven trap was a thatched cage that allowed fish to swim in, but not out.

52
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:33,260
The earliest known hooks, which date back to around 6,000 B.C., were not hooked at all.

53
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:34,940
They were called gorges.

54
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:41,220
Gorges, a definite link to the kind of earliest tackle available.

55
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:47,940
It's just a sharpened stone projectile at both ends with a groove in the center for a line.

56
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,980
The limitations of the gorge always revolved around the fact that the fish actually has to swallow the bait,

57
00:04:54,981 --> 00:04:58,840
has to take it all the way down into the throat, hence the name gorge.

58
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:02,720
Most fish don't feed as rapaciously as that implies.

59
00:05:02,721 --> 00:05:07,240
They will nibble at baits, they will bite at them, they'll pull at them to pull them away.

60
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,780
And as a result, most gorge baits, I'm sure, were lost.

61
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:17,900
I think the really first hooks began in the Copper Age, if you will, roughly 5000 B.C.

62
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,920
The curve in these early hooks put the angle in angling.

63
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:25,420
The angle is the hook.

64
00:05:25,421 --> 00:05:29,740
A lot of people think it's the rod and the reel, but the angle, the old word, literally means hook.

65
00:05:29,741 --> 00:05:36,300
Early line was fashioned by cutting thin strips of rawhide string from animal skins.

66
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:41,580
Some Egyptian anglers even used the hair of their dead,

67
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:43,860
which they braided together for greater strength.

68
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:49,320
For a couple of odd millennia, ancient anglers stood at the water's edge,

69
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:52,240
dangling their lines and baits in the water by hand.

70
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:57,140
No doubt these hand lines could be painful when bringing in a large fish.

71
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:01,300
Then sometime around 2500 B.C.,

72
00:06:01,301 --> 00:06:06,140
somebody got the bright idea that by tying their line to the narrow end of a limb or sapling,

73
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,920
they could extend their reach to deeper water,

74
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,620
and perhaps bigger fish.

75
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:18,060
One of the earliest depictions of rod and line fishing is this Egyptian painting,

76
00:06:18,061 --> 00:06:20,820
which dates back to around 2000 B.C.

77
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:26,340
Fighting a fish on a rod may have also introduced a new element into the mix.

78
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:29,520
Fishing for fun, or sport.

79
00:06:31,020 --> 00:06:35,380
Why else would Egyptian nobles, who certainly weren't worried about their next meal,

80
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,160
begin having private fishing ponds built in their palace gardens?

81
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:44,560
This new sport wasn't exclusively a guy thing.

82
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,220
There's a story involving Anthony and Cleopatra, told by Plutarch,

83
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:56,040
that involved Cleopatra's love of angling and Anthony's incompetence in this area, apparently.

84
00:06:56,041 --> 00:07:02,560
He, though, wanted to persuade her that he was adept at anything that she was interested in,

85
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:07,300
and so he arranged for divers to dive down and attach fish to his line

86
00:07:07,301 --> 00:07:11,060
so he would catch as many, if not more, than she each time they went fishing.

87
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,980
Cleopatra found out about this ruse of her lovers,

88
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:22,120
and she had one of her servants dive down with a fish that was already gutted, scaled, prepared,

89
00:07:22,121 --> 00:07:25,340
ready for the oven, and attached that to his line.

90
00:07:25,341 --> 00:07:30,380
And he reeled this in with a great deal of panache and landed it on the deck,

91
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:34,140
and everybody broke up great roars of laughter and embarrassment,

92
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:37,540
and that was the end of his fishing folly, shall we say.

93
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:43,760
For centuries, men and women continued to fish with line tied to the tip of the rod.

94
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:48,540
Horsehair had become the material of choice for most fishing line.

95
00:07:49,860 --> 00:07:54,720
The big drawback was, to make a line of any length, the hairs had to be knotted together.

96
00:07:55,340 --> 00:08:02,340
Around the 7th century BC, the Chinese came up with a better line made from worms.

97
00:08:02,341 --> 00:08:04,340
Silkworms, that is.

98
00:08:04,341 --> 00:08:07,340
The advantage of using silk lines, they have a high tensile strength.

99
00:08:07,341 --> 00:08:12,340
Another advantage of the silk lines is they were probably one long strand.

100
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:18,340
The quality of lines was vastly improved with the invention of looms and weaving machines.

101
00:08:18,341 --> 00:08:21,340
Better lines spawned more tackle innovation.

102
00:08:21,341 --> 00:08:28,340
By the 5th century, weights and plummets were cast from lead, and floats were carved from wood and cork.

103
00:08:28,341 --> 00:08:32,340
Double and treble hooks were being turned out by metal workers.

104
00:08:32,341 --> 00:08:38,340
These multi-point hooks increased an angler's chance of hooking and nibbling fish.

105
00:08:38,341 --> 00:08:46,340
By the 13th century, so much tackle was being invented that anglers needed some sort of container in which to carry it all.

106
00:08:46,341 --> 00:08:50,540
The first crude tackle pouches were made of leather or canvas.

107
00:08:52,500 --> 00:09:02,300
As more and more tackle began to appear, the pouches were replaced by woven creels that doubled as a tackle box and as a way to carry the angler's catch.

108
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:09,300
In the late 15th century, the first major writing on fishing tackle appeared in England.

109
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,440
It was the work of a dame.

110
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:16,140
Dame Juliana Berners was a remarkable woman.

111
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:21,000
She was active in field sports, certainly riding to hunt, falconry, things of this sort.

112
00:09:21,001 --> 00:09:30,060
What's interesting about Dame Juliana Berners, though, is that she did take the time to create this first treatise on field sports.

113
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:36,500
For the first time in print, at least, we see fishing regarded as recreation in the West, and that's an important milestone.

114
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:42,080
Berners wrote and drew extensively about fly tying.

115
00:09:42,440 --> 00:09:48,660
baits, line treatments, and hooks, but never mention the rod-mounted reel.

116
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:54,980
In fact, reels have been in existence since at least the 12th century.

117
00:09:56,200 --> 00:10:03,120
The earliest depiction of a rod-mounted winding reel is from a Chinese painting showing a man angling for turtles.

118
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:11,620
In the mid-1600s, Sir Isaac Walton published what would become the most famous book on angling and tackle,

119
00:10:11,621 --> 00:10:13,340
the complete angler.

120
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:18,880
Walton's writing was an encyclopedia of every kind of existing tackle,

121
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:20,380
including the reel.

122
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:23,680
These early winds, or winches as they were called,

123
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,980
were made of wood, tin, and brass.

124
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:29,300
They consisted of very simple hand-cranked drums

125
00:10:29,301 --> 00:10:31,260
set in a cage or housing.

126
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:37,200
By the 1800s, angling was beginning to catch on with the masses.

127
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:39,520
But casting was still difficult

128
00:10:39,521 --> 00:10:43,520
because these same reels were susceptible to something called backlash.

129
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:51,980
The spool travels faster than the speed of the line coming off of the reel,

130
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:54,740
and it balls up in a bird's nest kind of mess

131
00:10:54,741 --> 00:10:57,580
that usually takes a considerable amount of time to untangle.

132
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:00,340
One more level of innovation would be needed

133
00:11:00,341 --> 00:11:03,300
before angling could become as popular as other sports.

134
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:07,920
Next, the wheel story of how Yankee Ingenuity

135
00:11:07,921 --> 00:11:13,580
added the final piece to the modern angling rig and lured millions to the water's edge.

136
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:21,380
It is estimated that more than one million full-time jobs and over $100 billion of the

137
00:11:21,381 --> 00:11:24,474
American economy stem from the sport fishing industry.

138
00:11:24,475 --> 00:11:26,480
The Tacklebox will return on Modern

139
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:35,360
Marvels. The 20th century would see an explosion in the popularity of sport angling.

140
00:11:35,361 --> 00:11:36,100
But how

141
00:11:36,101 --> 00:11:41,100
How had this narrowly practiced pastime become one of the fastest growing sports in America?

142
00:11:41,101 --> 00:11:45,100
In a word, technology.

143
00:11:45,101 --> 00:11:52,600
About the time that Henry Ford's Model T was revolutionizing travel, an inventor fisherman

144
00:11:52,601 --> 00:11:59,280
named William Shakespeare of Kalamazoo, Michigan was doing the same thing for angling.

145
00:11:59,281 --> 00:12:04,780
Shakespeare patented an improved version of an earlier invention, the level-winding bait-casting

146
00:12:04,781 --> 00:12:05,780
reel.

147
00:12:05,781 --> 00:12:12,360
This is a Shakespeare level wind reel that is more or less the standard configuration of level winds.

148
00:12:12,361 --> 00:12:21,660
What a level wind does is when you retrieve the line, it evenly spools it or puts it on the reel evenly,

149
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:25,760
where in the past you would have to do it with your thumb and index finger.

150
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:33,440
These new reels allowed the occasional weekend angler to cast and retrieve with a minimum of practice.

151
00:12:33,441 --> 00:12:36,240
bait casting boomed.

152
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:40,080
Naturally, the growing number of bait casters

153
00:12:40,081 --> 00:12:43,080
increased the demand for something to cast, bait.

154
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:48,700
Bait is a fishing staple that has its own strange history.

155
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:55,440
Egyptians did use live rats in their angling techniques.

156
00:12:56,060 --> 00:12:58,500
The Nile perch can reach over 200 pounds,

157
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:01,360
so I guess they were trying to get some pretty big fish at that time

158
00:13:01,361 --> 00:13:02,480
using these live rats.

159
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:07,980
Some Greek anglers apparently tried wine to attract fish by scent.

160
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:13,720
If this seems odd, consider this more contemporary scent bait tale.

161
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:16,300
We caught blue marlin on WD-40 bait.

162
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:21,540
WD-40 on a bait started probably in the 60s when WD-40 first came out.

163
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:26,180
And the guys in the taco store would say, I've got a new secret, you can't tell anybody about it.

164
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:29,340
And they were spraying it on artificial lures for blue marlin fishing.

165
00:13:29,341 --> 00:13:34,340
Otter still are bait stories involving fast food and footwear.

166
00:13:34,341 --> 00:13:43,340
All these fishermen spent hours and bought all kinds of tackle trying to catch these big record-sized trout on Lake Tannicomo.

167
00:13:43,341 --> 00:13:51,340
Kid goes out, puts some McDonald's french fries on the end of his hook, and he sets the all-time lake record for rainbow trout.

168
00:13:51,341 --> 00:13:52,457
That had guys shook up.

169
00:13:52,458 --> 00:13:56,340
I don't know how many of them were sneaking around with french fries after that.

170
00:13:56,341 --> 00:13:58,320
that. Probably one of the craziest things I ever did

171
00:13:58,321 --> 00:14:00,300
were in St. Thomas, and the blue marlin

172
00:14:00,301 --> 00:14:01,580
are biting pretty good.

173
00:14:02,220 --> 00:14:04,160
So we took a hook and rigged a flip-flop.

174
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:06,360
Next blue marlin came up and crashed

175
00:14:06,361 --> 00:14:08,260
it, and we caught a 400-pound blue marlin

176
00:14:08,261 --> 00:14:08,961
on a flip-flop.

177
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:12,240
Most fish, however, tend to have

178
00:14:12,241 --> 00:14:14,060
less exotic, more predictable

179
00:14:14,061 --> 00:14:16,260
tastes. In the

180
00:14:16,261 --> 00:14:18,220
early 20s, the classic baits were

181
00:14:18,221 --> 00:14:19,560
seen as a cash crop.

182
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:22,280
Enterprising folks from all over

183
00:14:22,281 --> 00:14:24,080
the country began raising and selling

184
00:14:24,081 --> 00:14:26,260
minnows, crayfish, cricket,

185
00:14:26,340 --> 00:14:29,180
frogs, and of course, worms.

186
00:14:31,740 --> 00:14:34,980
At the Happy D Ranch in Visalia, California,

187
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:38,380
bait wranglers Carolyn Fox and Dorothy Benoy

188
00:14:38,381 --> 00:14:41,140
harvest and raise thousands of red wigglers.

189
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:49,620
The machine is a worm harvester or separator.

190
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:52,500
If we were to process all day long,

191
00:14:52,501 --> 00:14:57,860
We could probably process 200 to 250 pounds of worms in an eight-hour day.

192
00:15:03,740 --> 00:15:08,240
The red worms or red wigglers are terrific for fishing bait.

193
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:11,440
They move more than night crawlers do on a hook.

194
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:16,660
They are smaller than night crawlers, so some folks may want two worms on a hook at a time,

195
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:18,700
but they do make great fish bait.

196
00:15:22,500 --> 00:15:25,100
But what about the angler who wants to raise his own worms?

197
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:29,600
This is the famous can of worms.

198
00:15:29,740 --> 00:15:33,280
This is the most famous and used worm bin in the world.

199
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:37,180
Fishermen love this because they can grow their own worms in here.

200
00:15:37,181 --> 00:15:40,640
So they don't have to pay $2.50 per 20 or dozen.

201
00:15:41,060 --> 00:15:43,460
They can grow them by the thousands in this bin.

202
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:50,640
As bait fishing increased in popularity, it began to focus attention on the efficiency of hooks.

203
00:15:50,641 --> 00:15:55,840
In 1928, a young fly tire named Drew McGill

204
00:15:55,841 --> 00:15:58,760
began to study the mechanics of eagle talons

205
00:15:58,761 --> 00:16:03,400
and how their over-curved shape made them so effective in hooking their prey

206
00:16:03,401 --> 00:16:06,840
McGill began tinkering with the hooks of the day

207
00:16:06,841 --> 00:16:10,040
bending and twisting them out of shape with his fishing pliers

208
00:16:10,041 --> 00:16:13,780
He discovered that by offsetting the angle of the eye

209
00:16:13,781 --> 00:16:15,360
and aligning it with the point

210
00:16:15,361 --> 00:16:18,680
a geometrically perfect line of force was created

211
00:16:18,681 --> 00:16:23,360
McGill's new design revolutionized hook design around the world.

212
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:29,140
Today, Wright and McGill's Eagle Claw Fishing Tackle Company is one of the largest in the world

213
00:16:29,141 --> 00:16:31,520
and the only remaining hookmaker in America.

214
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:36,480
As tackle technology continued to improve,

215
00:16:37,020 --> 00:16:40,620
the American tackle box began to be invaded by a new kind of tackle.

216
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:44,180
Artificials. Man-made baits.

217
00:16:44,181 --> 00:16:51,080
Anglers have been using lures since ancient times, but the new dependable reels brought

218
00:16:51,081 --> 00:16:56,600
them into the, well, mainstream.

219
00:16:56,601 --> 00:17:02,420
One of the first and most basic lures of the modern era was the spinner or spoon.

220
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:06,500
Legend has it that its development was the result of a fortunate accident.

221
00:17:06,501 --> 00:17:07,201
J.T.

222
00:17:07,202 --> 00:17:13,980
Buell was having lunch in a boat, dropped a spoon over the side of the boat, and as

223
00:17:13,981 --> 00:17:17,060
the spoon fluttered down, the fish rose and hit the spoon.

224
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:19,980
He got the idea, gee, if I cut the handle off the spoon,

225
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:23,680
attach a hook to it, attach the line to a hook,

226
00:17:23,820 --> 00:17:27,760
and he was the first patented lure in the United States.

227
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:32,600
Lure maker Lou Eppinger took the spoon lure to the next level

228
00:17:32,601 --> 00:17:36,120
with the creation of his red and white daredevil spoon.

229
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:39,620
The daredevil spoon was a very, very popular spoon

230
00:17:39,621 --> 00:17:42,760
in the whole Northeast for catching all types of fish.

231
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:46,380
Spoons were predominantly sight lures.

232
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:51,540
Their underwater spinning motion simulated the flashing run of a fleeing bait fish.

233
00:17:53,080 --> 00:18:00,140
In 1902, James Hedden of Dowagiac, Michigan, created one of the first surface bass plugs.

234
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:04,780
Hedden called it a Dowagiac underwater or Dowagiac minnow.

235
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:10,800
Several of the surface lures were designed that on retrieve you would jerk the tip of the rod,

236
00:18:10,801 --> 00:18:15,080
causing the lure to splash water forward.

237
00:18:15,680 --> 00:18:21,080
As a predator fish, they were attracted and would hit any of those surface lures.

238
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:29,540
Fred Arbogast achieved lure-making immortality with the creation of his famous jitterbug.

239
00:18:31,020 --> 00:18:36,480
The jitterbug is still being made today, and the first bass I ever caught, I caught on a jitterbug.

240
00:18:36,481 --> 00:18:45,320
In the 1950s, the rubber or plastic worm bounced onto the scene and has remained a staple of freshwater fishing ever since.

241
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:54,020
In 1960, tackle dealers began importing a strange-looking lure from a firm in Finland called the Rapala Company.

242
00:18:55,080 --> 00:19:01,940
These sleek surface minnow plugs were hand-carved from balsa wood by the company's founder, Lowry Rapala.

243
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:07,100
What made the plug so deadly was an innovation called a diving lip.

244
00:19:07,880 --> 00:19:13,600
A plastic vein fixed in the lure's face, which caused the plug to dive when the angler retrieved it.

245
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:18,760
The faster the retrieve, the deeper the dive.

246
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:21,260
The fish ate it up.

247
00:19:24,460 --> 00:19:28,220
Lip lures to me is one of the most important innovations in fishing.

248
00:19:28,221 --> 00:19:32,460
And I believe Rapala was one of the very, very first who came out with a plastic lip.

249
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:38,000
A lip plug today is probably 60% of the total market of fishing lures.

250
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:45,440
To date, more freshwater records have been set and broken on lip lures than with any other design.

251
00:19:46,280 --> 00:19:48,260
But will they remain the kings forever?

252
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:54,380
Some anglers believe that one of the next big things is something called the boomerang.

253
00:19:55,340 --> 00:19:57,480
The boomerang isn't a lure at all.

254
00:19:57,481 --> 00:20:02,520
It's a rig that uses super strong bungee cord material to impart an extremely

255
00:20:02,521 --> 00:20:05,800
lifelike action to any lure or bait.

256
00:20:05,801 --> 00:20:11,340
What this is, is the world's first remote control device for fishing lowers.

257
00:20:11,341 --> 00:20:16,880
It allows the fisherman to fish in one location and his bait never stops moving.

258
00:20:16,881 --> 00:20:19,980
The bait actually follows the action of the rod tip.

259
00:20:19,981 --> 00:20:24,420
So he can fish for hours under a dock, piers, holes and fishes beds.

260
00:20:24,421 --> 00:20:28,020
You don't have to cast and reel, cast and reel, cast and reel.

261
00:20:28,021 --> 00:20:33,260
You allow the fish to come to the lure instead of you trying to throw to the fish.

262
00:20:33,261 --> 00:20:39,420
Back around 1940, as artificials gained popularity, a strange looking device began to appear on

263
00:20:39,421 --> 00:20:41,420
American streams.

264
00:20:41,421 --> 00:20:45,140
It was called the spinning or spin casting reel.

265
00:20:45,141 --> 00:20:50,100
Unlike the bait casting reel, the spinning reel's line spool didn't revolve.

266
00:20:50,101 --> 00:20:54,120
Instead, a spinning pickup bale rotated around the reciprocating spool

267
00:20:54,121 --> 00:20:56,600
and distributed line neatly and evenly.

268
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:04,680
Backlash and bird's nests, those nightmares of anglers everywhere, became virtually impossible.

269
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:12,140
Spinning reels revolutionized fishing from the standpoint that it made it easier for the common guy

270
00:21:12,141 --> 00:21:13,980
just to pick up a rod and reel and fish with.

271
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:18,020
Almost at the same time that the spinning reel was introduced,

272
00:21:18,021 --> 00:21:21,320
Another scientific breakthrough changed the world of angling forever.

273
00:21:22,380 --> 00:21:25,140
That breakthrough was nylon monofilament line.

274
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,960
The monofilament line did not come apart like the Dacron or the Linium lines.

275
00:21:30,300 --> 00:21:34,080
And this is why we had to have this type of line for this type of fishing reel.

276
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:38,880
While the new lines, lures, and reels got all the press,

277
00:21:39,480 --> 00:21:42,100
the unsung hero of modern angling remained,

278
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:45,580
the steel or aluminum box that had to carry all this stuff.

279
00:21:45,581 --> 00:21:50,580
But was anybody trying to make a better lighter box?

280
00:21:50,581 --> 00:21:58,260
In 1952, Pete Henning, founder of the Plano Moulding Company, returned from a fishing trip

281
00:21:58,261 --> 00:22:02,040
with a sore arm and an idea.

282
00:22:02,041 --> 00:22:06,460
Why not design and produce a line of plastic tackle boxes?

283
00:22:06,461 --> 00:22:10,340
The rest, as they say, is history.

284
00:22:10,341 --> 00:22:15,100
Today's lightweight tackle boxes are as sexy and high-tech as all the cool stuff inside

285
00:22:15,101 --> 00:22:16,100
them.

286
00:22:16,101 --> 00:22:21,780
And Plano is the largest maker of tackle boxes in the world.

287
00:22:21,781 --> 00:22:26,360
As the development of freshwater tackle was playing out on streams and lakes, similar

288
00:22:26,361 --> 00:22:31,400
innovations were making a splash in saltwater, where the catch was often larger than the

289
00:22:31,401 --> 00:22:32,400
angler.

290
00:22:32,401 --> 00:22:39,660
Next, the tackle box takes on the big game of the deep.

291
00:22:39,661 --> 00:22:44,420
Some of today's strongest and most advanced fishing lines, microfilaments, are made from

292
00:22:44,421 --> 00:22:48,540
materials like Kevlar, which is used to make bulletproof vests.

293
00:22:48,541 --> 00:22:51,560
The Tacklebox will return on Modern Marvels.

294
00:22:51,561 --> 00:22:58,500
Today's big game deep sea angling is like freshwater fishing on steroids.

295
00:22:58,501 --> 00:23:01,840
On the stream or lake, the emphasis is on the bite.

296
00:23:01,841 --> 00:23:03,980
Out here, things are different.

297
00:23:03,981 --> 00:23:08,140
The catch often outweighs the catcher.

298
00:23:08,141 --> 00:23:12,500
The bite is only the beginning of a fight to the finish that may last hours.

299
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:17,440
The sheer scale of the sea adds a sense of mystery and danger.

300
00:23:18,660 --> 00:23:22,640
There's something about the sea, being on the sea itself, the fact that it's unexpected.

301
00:23:22,900 --> 00:23:26,700
You go to the ocean, you have absolutely no idea what surprises there may be,

302
00:23:26,780 --> 00:23:28,440
and there always are some surprises.

303
00:23:28,441 --> 00:23:38,500
where freshwater angling began in europe saltwater sport fishing got its start in the new world

304
00:23:38,501 --> 00:23:45,340
with the arrival of its first true champion the year was 1831 sport fishing in america

305
00:23:45,341 --> 00:23:51,340
really begins with a man named henry william herbert herbert was an english outdoor writer

306
00:23:51,341 --> 00:23:55,760
with a shady past he had been disowned by his family and sent to the new world

307
00:23:55,761 --> 00:24:00,220
The fishing abundance of the New York coast amazed and inspired him.

308
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:05,360
But he arrived at a time when ocean fishing was the domain of the commercial fleets.

309
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:11,540
Large hull nets and multi-hook hand lines were being used to drag in fish by the ton.

310
00:24:13,100 --> 00:24:15,720
Herbert, writing under the alias Frank Forrester,

311
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:21,300
was the first major voice to argue that ocean fish could and should be fished for sport,

312
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:24,560
using a single hook on a rod and reel.

313
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:30,740
By the end of the Civil War, Herbert's ideas had a small but fanatical following.

314
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:36,480
This new breed cut their teeth on striped bass and bluefish,

315
00:24:37,040 --> 00:24:39,540
then looked around for a real saltwater challenge.

316
00:24:40,540 --> 00:24:43,960
Their search took them to the coast and inland waterways of Florida.

317
00:24:45,480 --> 00:24:51,180
The tarpon was really the first quarry of saltwater sports fishermen back in the 1800s.

318
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:55,180
They traveled specifically to Florida to catch the tarpon.

319
00:24:55,181 --> 00:24:59,000
A tarpon would run anywhere from 40 to 200 pounds.

320
00:24:59,160 --> 00:25:04,340
A spectacular jumper and an extra hard fighter, just a wonderful game fish.

321
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:11,700
Herbert's disciples got a rude awakening when they started hooking tarpon on tackle they had used for stripers and salmon.

322
00:25:12,740 --> 00:25:17,120
Tackle the day they used to catch the tarpon was highly inadequate.

323
00:25:17,121 --> 00:25:20,880
but the tarpon was a much stronger fish and would fight a lot harder

324
00:25:20,881 --> 00:25:25,560
and could actually drag the angler around the water for hours

325
00:25:25,561 --> 00:25:27,440
while he tried to land the fish.

326
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:33,040
The direct drive brakeless reels of that period were no match for tarpon

327
00:25:33,041 --> 00:25:35,160
or any other big game fish.

328
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:38,400
So they invented kind of a leather thumb drag

329
00:25:38,401 --> 00:25:40,760
where they would press their thumb against this leather

330
00:25:40,761 --> 00:25:45,960
which would then press against the line to retard the line paying off the reel.

331
00:25:47,120 --> 00:25:53,080
Reel makers experimented with various braking systems that could slow the run of large game fish.

332
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:59,300
The first major breakthrough was something called the star drag system, which is still in use today.

333
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:07,540
The star drag system was really the first drag system developed that made it easy for the angler to adjust the drag on the reel,

334
00:26:07,760 --> 00:26:13,120
to adjust the tension and the amount of line that was going out while he was playing the fish.

335
00:26:13,121 --> 00:26:19,520
The star drag was a threaded mechanical brake that the angler controlled with his thumb.

336
00:26:20,800 --> 00:26:23,540
Tightening the star wheel put pressure against the reel's spool

337
00:26:23,541 --> 00:26:26,340
and put the brakes on a running fish.

338
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:32,460
By the late 1800s, saltwater rods and reels had improved to the point

339
00:26:32,461 --> 00:26:34,280
where they could handle the largest tarpon.

340
00:26:35,240 --> 00:26:39,560
Then, something happened in California that raised the bar to new heights.

341
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:41,680
The year was 1898.

342
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:49,700
Dr. Charles Frederick Holder in Pasadena, California, took a trip out to Avalon,

343
00:26:49,701 --> 00:26:57,040
and he was watching people fish with hand lines and ropes and giant hooks

344
00:26:57,041 --> 00:27:02,820
in an unsportsmanlike way for tuna, and he thought he'd give a try at fishing with rod and reel,

345
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:09,900
and he was credited with catching the first bluefin tuna on rod and reel in 1898 in Avalon, California.

346
00:27:09,901 --> 00:27:18,560
big game sport fishing was a hit but early anglers still had some painful lessons to learn about their tackle

347
00:27:18,561 --> 00:27:24,680
some early direct drive reels of the day had earned themselves the inglorious nickname

348
00:27:24,681 --> 00:27:31,460
of knuckle busters one of the big problems was when the line was paying out when the fish was

349
00:27:31,461 --> 00:27:38,960
running the line off the handle on the reel would spin backwards now an angler would try and put his

350
00:27:38,961 --> 00:27:42,840
hand in there and grab the handle to slow the fish down to stop the fish and

351
00:27:42,841 --> 00:27:46,800
oftentimes knuckles were broken, fingers were broken, and

352
00:27:46,801 --> 00:27:53,540
fishermen were injured. In the late 1800s, deep sea reel makers like

353
00:27:53,541 --> 00:27:59,860
Kavaloski in California and the Womhoffs in New York began designing reels that

354
00:27:59,861 --> 00:28:03,540
would allow the spool to turn independently of the handle while the

355
00:28:03,541 --> 00:28:08,300
fish was running.

356
00:28:08,301 --> 00:28:12,880
By the early 20th century, the sport that Henry William Herbert had started was becoming

357
00:28:12,881 --> 00:28:17,580
the pastime of the rich and famous.

358
00:28:17,581 --> 00:28:21,860
One major contributor to the sport's popularity was also a writer.

359
00:28:21,861 --> 00:28:26,060
One of the most remarkable men involved in the early history of saltwater angling was

360
00:28:26,061 --> 00:28:27,060
Zane Gray.

361
00:28:27,061 --> 00:28:28,640
He was a very popular writer.

362
00:28:28,641 --> 00:28:35,480
At one point, his book sold better than any other writer except the McGuffey Readers and perhaps the Holy Bible.

363
00:28:35,900 --> 00:28:38,180
He was one of the founders of Paramount Studios.

364
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:42,620
And he was a great publicist on behalf of Anglin.

365
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:48,360
Another writer of the day who loved ocean fishing was a fellow named Ernest Hemingway.

366
00:28:49,320 --> 00:28:53,460
Hemingway was using the same tackle that everybody else was using at the time.

367
00:28:53,461 --> 00:28:58,220
What made him successful where everybody else failed was his technique.

368
00:28:58,640 --> 00:29:03,680
Hemingway was very aggressive and he said that if you battled the fish, if you beat the fish,

369
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:05,380
then the fish wouldn't beat you.

370
00:29:05,381 --> 00:29:11,940
And he was the first person to be successful in 1935 landing at a giant bluefin tuna in Bimini.

371
00:29:13,400 --> 00:29:18,600
Along with a love of big game angling, Gray and Hemingway shared a deep hatred of sharks

372
00:29:18,601 --> 00:29:21,260
that would tear a big fish to shreds during the fight.

373
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:27,180
Hemingway, his response was to simply tackle the shark problem head on as he saw it.

374
00:29:27,181 --> 00:29:33,940
He carried a machine gun aboard his boat, and every opportunity he could take, he would pour lead into these fish.

375
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:38,640
The shark factor increased the need to tire and boat fish more quickly.

376
00:29:39,420 --> 00:29:42,140
This started a new demand for stronger rods.

377
00:29:42,760 --> 00:29:48,960
Up until this point, most of the rods that were being used were made of hickory and green heart.

378
00:29:48,961 --> 00:29:58,720
In the 1940s, a material was developed that would make lightweight tubular rods that were stronger than solid wood or tubular steel.

379
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:01,440
That material was fiberglass.

380
00:30:03,100 --> 00:30:11,960
By the 1950s, big game anglers using improved drag reels and fiberglass rods were consistently catching marlin, tuna, and swordfish.

381
00:30:14,180 --> 00:30:17,520
Today, fiberglass rods remain extremely popular.

382
00:30:18,960 --> 00:30:25,720
At the Rodco Manufacturing Company in Santa Ana, California, fiberglass impregnated material

383
00:30:25,721 --> 00:30:33,520
is rolled out into sheets and then cut into precise patterns.

384
00:30:33,521 --> 00:30:47,521
Through a series of exacting steps, this flimsy material will be rolled, baked, painted,

385
00:30:47,580 --> 00:30:50,900
appointed

386
00:30:50,901 --> 00:30:55,260
and packaged.

387
00:30:57,400 --> 00:31:00,700
The rods are then shipped off to the local tackle shop

388
00:31:00,701 --> 00:31:04,660
and eventually out to sea and into action.

389
00:31:07,480 --> 00:31:10,080
Next, we'll hang up our deep sea broomsticks

390
00:31:10,081 --> 00:31:12,760
and take down the magic wand of the fly fisherman

391
00:31:12,761 --> 00:31:16,420
as the tackle box becomes the tackle vest.

392
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:25,440
Most sources believe that the fastest fish in the sea is the Indo-Pacific sailfish,

393
00:31:25,441 --> 00:31:29,860
which has been clocked in excess of 68 miles per hour over short distances.

394
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:32,980
The Tackle Box will return on Modern Marvels.

395
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:38,440
We now return to the Tackle Box on Modern Marvels.

396
00:31:38,441 --> 00:31:50,740
The physics involved in what we call fly angling or fly casting is unlike any other form of casting.

397
00:31:50,741 --> 00:31:53,380
In fly fishing, you cast the weight of the line.

398
00:31:54,960 --> 00:32:00,460
In all other kinds of fishing involving casting something, you're casting the weight of the lure or the bait.

399
00:32:00,461 --> 00:32:08,900
Fly fishing goes back a long way in human history.

400
00:32:09,680 --> 00:32:13,880
Egyptian paintings showed six lines dangling from a pole,

401
00:32:13,881 --> 00:32:17,120
and each of those lines had an artificial fly attached to it.

402
00:32:17,640 --> 00:32:20,460
So probably the Egyptians were the first fly anglers.

403
00:32:21,380 --> 00:32:24,780
Modern fly fishing began in England in 1496.

404
00:32:25,880 --> 00:32:28,740
Once again, it was Dame Juliana Berners.

405
00:32:28,741 --> 00:32:34,460
Dame Juliana Berners was probably the first amateur aquatic entomologist.

406
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:40,380
By studying insects, she realized that certain fish, particularly trout, feed upon them,

407
00:32:40,800 --> 00:32:44,300
and she started to develop flies that imitated these insects.

408
00:32:46,440 --> 00:32:49,420
At the core of fly casting lives the question,

409
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:53,420
how can I cast a lure that is lighter than air across a stream?

410
00:32:53,421 --> 00:32:59,520
The answer? Fly line is much thicker and heavier than monofilament.

411
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:03,060
Line used for wet flies is designed to sink.

412
00:33:03,640 --> 00:33:06,880
Line used with dry flies has a special coating that allows it to float.

413
00:33:07,800 --> 00:33:14,820
The fact that the weight is in the line, not in the lure, made fly tackle different from the ground, or should we say, from the rod up.

414
00:33:15,880 --> 00:33:20,740
Finding the best rod material that could cast this special line, however, didn't happen overnight.

415
00:33:20,741 --> 00:33:27,740
Starting in the 15th century, they started experimenting with different woods, hickory,

416
00:33:27,741 --> 00:33:31,740
willow, lemon woods, and later on they used green heart.

417
00:33:31,741 --> 00:33:37,740
The weight of the solid woods tired the arm in a matter of hours and sent rod makers

418
00:33:37,741 --> 00:33:40,740
on a search for material that was limber, light and strong.

419
00:33:40,741 --> 00:33:48,740
In the early part of the 19th century, they experimented with strips of bamboo that were

420
00:33:48,741 --> 00:33:50,010
glued together.

421
00:33:50,011 --> 00:33:56,700
In 1867, Hiram Leonard developed a six-sectional rod, which became the standard

422
00:33:56,701 --> 00:34:03,960
and is still employed today in modern bamboo manufacturing.

423
00:34:06,020 --> 00:34:10,520
In spite of all bamboo's virtues, it did have an important downside.

424
00:34:11,180 --> 00:34:14,187
Bamboo manufacturing is labor-intensive.

425
00:34:14,188 --> 00:34:18,020
You have to cure it, strip it, and put it together.

426
00:34:18,021 --> 00:34:25,520
This is a labor-intensive time frame of months to get it to where it can be usable to fish with.

427
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:35,400
The extensive labor involved in producing split cane rods began to price them out of the mainstream market.

428
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:41,860
Bamboo rods today are very expensive, and they're very exquisite instruments.

429
00:34:41,861 --> 00:34:48,780
These rods can cost between $2,000 and $3,000, and they'll take anywhere from a year to four years to get one.

430
00:34:50,100 --> 00:34:54,920
In the 1940s, fiberglass was introduced as an alternative to bamboo.

431
00:34:56,480 --> 00:34:59,560
Glass rods, as they're called, are still very popular today.

432
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:03,500
The next generation of fly rods came from the heavens.

433
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:07,320
Aerospace has impacted our sport of fishing.

434
00:35:07,321 --> 00:35:12,280
The rods that we use, these are made out of graphite materials which have come out of the aerospace industry.

435
00:35:12,700 --> 00:35:17,020
They're light, they're module, they have a tremendous amount of strength and flexibility.

436
00:35:20,260 --> 00:35:23,840
At the St. Croix Rod Building plant in Park Falls, Wisconsin,

437
00:35:24,500 --> 00:35:30,320
the average rod moves through approximately 36 pairs of human hands from first step to last.

438
00:35:30,321 --> 00:35:35,960
Rods that are 9 feet in length can weigh as little as 3 ounces

439
00:35:35,961 --> 00:35:40,180
and still land a 6 to 10 pound trout in a fast moving stream

440
00:35:40,181 --> 00:35:47,020
Fly casting not only changed tackle, it changed technique

441
00:35:47,021 --> 00:35:50,720
By virtue of its backward and forward casting motion

442
00:35:50,721 --> 00:35:54,160
it forced the angler off the bank and out into the water

443
00:35:54,161 --> 00:36:01,160
Waiters became a staple of the serious fly fisherman.

444
00:36:01,161 --> 00:36:07,160
It changed the tackle box from a large container that you plunk down on the bank to a piece of apparel.

445
00:36:07,161 --> 00:36:11,160
The tackle box of a fly fisherman

446
00:36:11,161 --> 00:36:23,160
put fly boxes in, leaders, tippet material, tools that he would be using such as clippers,

447
00:36:23,161 --> 00:36:26,320
such as clippers.

448
00:36:26,321 --> 00:36:31,840
With all this high-tech gear, such as ultralight rods and floating lines, it's no wonder that

449
00:36:31,841 --> 00:36:37,980
fly fishermen sometimes see themselves as a special breed.

450
00:36:37,981 --> 00:36:45,140
The Fisherman's Spot in Van Nuys, California is a mecca for West Coast fly fishermen.

451
00:36:45,141 --> 00:36:48,560
These guys are serious.

452
00:36:48,561 --> 00:36:55,560
At the center of this temple of trout sits the spiritual master, the fly tire.

453
00:36:57,560 --> 00:37:04,560
The challenge of any fly tire is to take these materials and make it have a silhouette or

454
00:37:04,561 --> 00:37:09,560
shadow, some type of light pattern to match what the fish would naturally be feeding on.

455
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:15,560
The fly tire uses some strange sounding techniques in his craft.

456
00:37:15,561 --> 00:37:19,720
Applying the yarn to the hook to create the body of the fly is called dubbing.

457
00:37:24,260 --> 00:37:28,340
Palmering is a method of winding thread to make the hackles simulate wings.

458
00:37:31,060 --> 00:37:34,560
As the master works, an insect begins to take shape.

459
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:39,080
Then what you're going to do is finish the fly with what we call the whip finisher.

460
00:37:39,081 --> 00:37:42,680
and that just is a tool that uses to finish the fly,

461
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:45,660
helps lock the thread down and prevent the fly from coming apart.

462
00:37:49,540 --> 00:37:52,460
And then you're going to add just a slight bit of head cement

463
00:37:52,461 --> 00:37:56,000
to hold the thread in place so the fly doesn't come apart on you.

464
00:37:57,080 --> 00:37:58,240
